From: NanoKVM BSP <nanokvm@local>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: sophgo: nanokvm: enable i2c4 for the LT6911C

The HDMI-to-CSI bridge that feeds the capture pipeline is controlled over
i2c4 at 0x2b -- it is how the input resolution is read and the EDID written.
Mainline leaves i2c@4040000 disabled and there is no upstream pinctrl for it,
so nothing in the running system can reach the bridge: the board comes up with
a single i2c bus (the slave EEPROM on i2c@4010000).

The pad choice and the alias handling are both load-bearing and both are
explained inline in the DTS rather than here, because that is where anyone
changing them will be looking.

Must follow 0003 and 0011 -- it appends to the same board .dts they edit.

Not upstream -- local to this build.
---
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2002-licheerv-nano-b.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2002-licheerv-nano-b.dts
@@ -255,3 +255,58 @@
 &cvi_vc_drv {
 	status = "okay";
 };
+
+/*
+ * i2c4 -- the control bus for the LT6911C HDMI-to-CSI bridge (0x2b).
+ *
+ * The pads are not a free choice. IIC4 can be routed to five different pad
+ * pairs on this SoC, and Sipeed's board init picks one by raw MMIO rather than
+ * by DT (LicheeRV-Nano-Build, build/boards/sg200x/sg2002_licheervnano_sd/
+ * u-boot/cvi_board_init.c):
+ *
+ *	mmio_write_32(0x03001090, 0x5);		// IIC4_SCL
+ *	mmio_write_32(0x03001098, 0x5);		// IIC4_SDA
+ *
+ * 0x090 and 0x098 are the FUNCSEL registers of PWR_WAKEUP0 and PWR_BUTTON1 in
+ * mainline's own pinctrl-sg2002.c, and CVITek's pad table agrees on the
+ * function number: PWR_WAKEUP0__IIC4_SCL = 5, PWR_BUTTON1__IIC4_SDA = 5.
+ * There is no vendor pinctrl node to copy -- the vendor BSP muxes every pad
+ * from a generated table before Linux starts -- so it is spelled out here.
+ *
+ * These are the RTC-domain power-button/wakeup pads; muxing them to IIC4 gives
+ * up those functions. They are IO_TYPE_1V8_ONLY, so there is deliberately no
+ * power-source property: pinctrl-cv18xx ignores it ("always fixed").
+ */
+&pinctrl {
+	i2c4_cfg: i2c4-cfg {
+		i2c4-pins {
+			pinmux = <PINMUX(PIN_PWR_WAKEUP0, 5)>,
+				 <PINMUX(PIN_PWR_BUTTON1, 5)>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&i2c4 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+/*
+ * Pin BOTH bus numbers, not just i2c4. i2c_init() does
+ * of_alias_get_highest_id("i2c") and starts *dynamic* numbering above it, so
+ * declaring i2c4 alone would push the slave-EEPROM controller (i2c@4010000)
+ * up to bus 5, and moving it again later would move its sysfs path with it.
+ * i2c0 = &i2c1 is not a typo: it holds that controller at bus 0 so
+ * /sys/bus/i2c/devices/0-1050 stays where every debugging note says it is,
+ * while i2c4 = &i2c4 puts the bridge at /dev/i2c-4 where the vendor EDID
+ * tooling looks for it.
+ *
+ * Nothing on the BMC reads the EEPROM today -- the managed host writes it and
+ * the samples are consumed there -- so the number is a stability choice, not
+ * a functional dependency.
+ */
+/ {
+	aliases {
+		i2c0 = &i2c1;
+		i2c4 = &i2c4;
+	};
+};
